# Environment for docker-compose.prod.yml and docker-compose.backoffice.yml.
# Copy to .env NEXT TO those files and fill in real values. Never commit the result -- it holds every secret in the
# stack. Every changeme-... value below is a placeholder: deploy.sh refuses to
# start the stack while any setting still holds one, and names each.
#
# This file plus the two compose files is everything a production host needs.
# The same pair runs the stack locally; only the values below change, which is
# what makes "test exactly what you are about to deploy" true rather than
# aspirational. Local values are given beside each key that differs.

#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Images
#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Version tag for BOTH ghcr.io/jakefearsd/pressroom and
# ghcr.io/jakefearsd/pressroom-caddy. They are published together from one release
# job and must move together. Published only when a v*.*.* tag is pushed
# (.github/workflows/release.yml), so this is a release, never "whatever was on
# master".
#
# NOTE: GHCR packages are private on first publish. Until the package is made
# public (GitHub -> Packages -> Settings -> Change visibility) or you
# `docker login ghcr.io` here with a PAT carrying read:packages, an anonymous
# pull fails with "denied".
IMAGE_VERSION=0.8.8

# Extra JVM flags for the app container, e.g. -Xmx1g. Optional.
JAVA_OPTS=

#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Compose files
#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# The files plain `docker compose` reads from this directory (Compose takes
# COMPOSE_FILE from .env itself; no -f needed on any command). The core file
# runs Pressroom; the back-office overlay runs the services it uses -- Umami
# and listmonk -- until they run with the back-office suite (wiki SA-15).
# Then drop the overlay from this line, and point ANALYTICS_UPSTREAM,
# NEWSLETTER_UPSTREAM, PRESSROOM_ANALYTICS_UMAMI_APIURL and
# PRESSROOM_NEWSLETTER_LISTMONK_BASEURL at the suite (docker_deployment.md,
# "The back-office services"). deploy.sh adds this line to a .env without one.
COMPOSE_FILE=docker-compose.prod.yml:docker-compose.backoffice.yml

#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Public domain / TLS
#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Your real domain. Caddy requests and auto-renews a Let's Encrypt certificate
# for it; you need only DNS pointed here and 80/443 reachable (80 carries the
# ACME challenge).
#
# LOCAL: use ":80". A bare port is not a certificate-able name, so automatic
# HTTPS never activates and Caddy serves plain HTTP.
#
# Deliberately NOT named PRESSROOM_DOMAIN: that prefix is reserved for app
# configuration (see below), and a stray PRESSROOM_DOMAIN would be overlaid onto
# the app's properties as a junk key named "domain".
SITE_DOMAIN=blog.example.com

# Host ports Caddy publishes. Unset, they are 80 and 443, which a public host
# needs (80 carries the ACME challenge). Move them when this host sits behind
# a tunnel connector or reverse proxy on ANOTHER machine (e.g. cloudflared)
# and 80/443 belong to something else: point the connector at
# http://<this host>:SITE_HTTP_PORT and leave SITE_DOMAIN at ":80". Only the
# host side moves; inside the container Caddy still listens on 80 and 443.
#SITE_HTTP_PORT=80
#SITE_HTTPS_PORT=443

# The proxy on another machine whose X-Forwarded-For and X-Forwarded-Proto
# Caddy keeps: its address, or several separated by spaces (CIDR ranges
# work too). Unset, Caddy trusts none and replaces both headers, which a
# public host needs; behind a connector it means every reader reaches the
# app as the connector's address, over plain HTTP. Name that one machine,
# never a whole private range. A malformed value stops Caddy, and the site.
#TRUSTED_PROXIES=192.168.1.50

# Where Pressroom sits on that domain. Unset (the default) it serves from the
# ROOT: a weblog is https://<domain>/<handle>/, and nothing in a canonical url,
# og:url, sitemap <loc> or feed id carries a path prefix that would later cost
# a redirect map to remove.
#
# Set this ONLY to share the hostname with another app, or to preserve the urls
# of an installation that already ran under /roller (the default before 0.1.4 -- rebrand:keep: the real pre-0.1.4 URL
# see docker_deployment.md, "The context path", for the Caddy redirect to move
# such a site to the root instead). It is a Spring Boot property rather than a
# PRESSROOM_* one, reached by relaxed binding, because the context path belongs to
# the servlet container and not to Pressroom's own configuration.
#SERVER_SERVLET_CONTEXT_PATH=/roller # rebrand:keep: the real pre-0.1.4 URL

# Per-weblog custom domains (virtual hosting): the DNS zone Caddy holds a
# WILDCARD certificate for, e.g. "thelocalwiki.com" for a *.thelocalwiki.com
# cert covering every subdomain under it with no per-blog deploy step. A
# wildcard can only be proven via the ACME DNS-01 challenge -- unlike
# SITE_DOMAIN above, this is not automatic; it needs the Caddy image built
# with a DNS provider module (see deploy/caddy/Dockerfile) and the token
# below. Leave both unset and this feature is simply inert -- SITE_DOMAIN
# keeps working exactly as before. Full procedure (this variable, the
# pressroom.properties `vhost.cert.zones` warning-only allowlist on the app
# side, the DNS record, then setting the domain on Weblog Settings):
# docker_deployment.md, "Custom domains (virtual hosting)".
#
# IMPORTANT: the moment ANY weblog has a custom domain, PRESSROOM_SITE_ABSOLUTEURL
# (below, under "Pressroom application configuration") becomes required too --
# see docker_deployment.md's step 0 for why leaving it unset misattributes
# every domain-less weblog's canonical urls to whichever host happens to hit
# the server first after boot.
#VHOST_ZONE=thelocalwiki.com

# API token for the DNS-01 challenge against VHOST_ZONE, scoped to that DNS
# provider account. deploy/caddy/Dockerfile bakes in the Cloudflare provider
# module (github.com/caddy-dns/cloudflare), so this must be a Cloudflare API
# token (My Profile -> API Tokens -> Create Token) with Zone:DNS:Edit on
# VHOST_ZONE and nothing more -- it never needs to touch anything else in the
# account. Building against a different DNS provider means changing both this
# comment and the module `xcaddy build --with`s in the Dockerfile together.
#VHOST_DNS_API_TOKEN=changeme-cloudflare-api-token

#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Pressroom application configuration
#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Every PRESSROOM_* variable here is overlaid onto the app's built-in defaults at
# startup: strip the prefix, lowercase, turn _ into . -- so
# PRESSROOM_MAIL_HOSTNAME sets the property mail.hostname. This is the
# highest-precedence configuration layer, and it is the ONLY one the production
# image uses; there is no properties file to mount. Any key documented in
# app/src/main/resources/com/wikantik/pressroom/config/pressroom.properties
# can be set this way.

# Schema handling. 'auto' applies no migration itself: the provision service
# has already applied every pending one by then. If one is still pending (a
# failed provision), the app does not start and every request, public pages
# included, lands on the install wizard. ('manual', the built-in default,
# would instead apply pending migrations silently at startup.)
PRESSROOM_INSTALLATION_TYPE=auto

# Database. The URL's host is the compose service name; username and password
# must match POSTGRES_USER/POSTGRES_PASSWORD below, and the trailing path
# segment (the database name, "pressroom") must match POSTGRES_DB below too --
# none of these three are templated against each other. Change POSTGRES_DB
# alone and provision migrates a database under the new name while this URL
# still points at the old, unmigrated one.
PRESSROOM_DATABASE_CONFIGURATIONTYPE=jdbc
PRESSROOM_DATABASE_JDBC_DRIVERCLASS=org.postgresql.Driver
PRESSROOM_DATABASE_JDBC_CONNECTIONURL=jdbc:postgresql://postgres:5432/pressroom
PRESSROOM_DATABASE_JDBC_USERNAME=pressroom
PRESSROOM_DATABASE_JDBC_PASSWORD=changeme-generate-a-real-secret

# Storage paths. These match the image's own layout and the volumes mounted by
# docker-compose.prod.yml; there is no reason to change them.
PRESSROOM_THEMES_DIR=/app/themes
PRESSROOM_MEDIAFILES_STORAGE_DIR=/data/mediafiles
PRESSROOM_SEARCH_INDEX_DIR=/data/search-index
PRESSROOM_UPLOADS_DIR=/data/uploads

# Per-weblog custom domains (virtual hosting): comma-separated apex names the
# proxy holds a wildcard certificate for (see VHOST_ZONE above -- the two must
# name the same zone). Advisory only: saving a weblog's custom domain outside
# these zones on Weblog Settings produces a warning, nothing more. Startup
# property, not a runtime one -- unlike most PRESSROOM_* settings here, changing
# it needs a restart. Blank (the default) warns about nothing.
#PRESSROOM_VHOST_CERT_ZONES=thelocalwiki.com

# The site's own absolute address, e.g. https://blog.example.com -- REQUIRED
# the moment any weblog has a custom domain (see VHOST_ZONE above and
# docker_deployment.md's "Custom domains (virtual hosting)" step 0). Left
# blank, the app falls back to whichever hostname happens to hit the server
# first after boot, which under virtual hosting can be a custom domain --
# misattributing every domain-less weblog's canonical url, og:url, feed id,
# sitemap, robots.txt and password-reset links to it. It is also a runtime
# property (Admin -> Global Config -> "Absolute URL to site"): a value saved
# on that page wins over this variable, and while that field is blank (its
# default) this variable is used, so it can be set without an admin ever
# visiting the page.
#PRESSROOM_SITE_ABSOLUTEURL=https://blog.example.com

# Mail: your SMTP relay. Used for password-reset and notification mail.
# Username and password are optional -- set them only if your relay needs auth.
# Reset mail ALSO needs the "Site Administrator's email address" (Admin -> Global Config, the
# runtime property site.adminemail): it is the From address of every mail.
# Worked example, Brevo (the From address must be a verified sender/domain):
#   PRESSROOM_MAIL_HOSTNAME=smtp-relay.brevo.com
#   PRESSROOM_MAIL_PORT=587
#   PRESSROOM_MAIL_USERNAME=<Brevo SMTP login>
#   PRESSROOM_MAIL_PASSWORD=<Brevo SMTP key>
# mail.security defaults to starttls. docker_deployment.md, "Mail and account
# recovery".
PRESSROOM_MAIL_CONFIGURATIONTYPE=properties
PRESSROOM_MAIL_HOSTNAME=smtp.example.com
PRESSROOM_MAIL_PORT=587
#PRESSROOM_MAIL_USERNAME=
#PRESSROOM_MAIL_PASSWORD=

# Newsletter: where /newsletter/subscribe forwards to. The APP serves that
# endpoint (throttle + pressroom_event recording) and calls listmonk directly
# -- Caddy has no route for it and must never be given one. Unset, the
# back-office overlay points it at http://listmonk:9000, the listmonk on this
# host; set it to listmonk's LAN address once listmonk runs elsewhere. Blank
# leaves the endpoint returning 503.
#PRESSROOM_NEWSLETTER_LISTMONK_BASEURL=http://listmonk:9000

# Higher-privilege credentials for the entry editor's "Send as newsletter"
# button (a listmonk API user, Admin -> Users -> + New, Type: API -- see
# docker_deployment.md's Newsletter section for how to create one). Separate
# from LISTMONK_BASEURL above: leaving either of these blank disables only
# the Send-as-newsletter button, the public subscribe form keeps working
# regardless.
#PRESSROOM_NEWSLETTER_LISTMONK_APIUSER=
#PRESSROOM_NEWSLETTER_LISTMONK_APITOKEN=

#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Postgres credentials
#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Shared by postgres, provision, and backup. Keep POSTGRES_USER/PASSWORD in
# sync with PRESSROOM_DATABASE_JDBC_USERNAME/PASSWORD above, and POSTGRES_DB in
# sync with the database name in PRESSROOM_DATABASE_JDBC_CONNECTIONURL above --
# none of these are templated against each other, and changing POSTGRES_DB
# alone makes provision migrate one database while the app connects to
# another.
#
# Generate the password URL-safe:
#     openssl rand -base64 33 | tr '+/' '-_'
# (44 characters, no padding). docker-compose.prod.yml splices it into
# Umami's DATABASE_URL (postgresql://user:PASSWORD@postgres/...), so a "/"
# from plain base64 breaks Umami. Images up to 0.1.10 also refuse an
# even-length all-hex password (openssl rand -hex N) with EclipseLink-7360;
# see docker_deployment.md, Troubleshooting.
POSTGRES_DB=pressroom
POSTGRES_USER=pressroom
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=changeme-generate-a-real-secret

#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Nightly backup service
#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Hour (UTC, 0-23) for pg_dump + volume snapshots. A bare number is clearest,
# though zero-padded is parsed correctly too.
BACKUP_HOUR=3
BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS=14

# Umami analytics older than this many days are deleted every night, straight
# after the backup (so the backup taken just before still holds them). 730 is
# two years, what the privacy policy promises; 0 switches deletion off.
#UMAMI_RETENTION_DAYS=730

#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Analytics (Umami)
#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Self-hosted and cookie-free, so no consent banner is required. The tracker
# and its collect endpoint are served from the BLOG's own origin at
# /analytics/* -- every bundled theme sends script-src 'self' and connect-src
# 'self', so a tracker on any other hostname is blocked and records nothing.
#
# Signing key for Umami's session tokens. Changing it logs everyone out.
#     openssl rand -hex 32
UMAMI_APP_SECRET=changeme-openssl-rand-hex-32

# Only the tracker is public: /analytics/<UMAMI_SCRIPT_NAME> and
# POST /analytics/api/send. Every other /analytics/ path answers 404, and the
# Umami DASHBOARD and its API are on port 8081 of this host
# (http://localhost:8081 locally), never under the blog's name.
#
# The address that port is published on. Unset, 127.0.0.1: reachable from
# this host only. To give the dashboard a hostname of its own (a tunnel
# route or proxy on ANOTHER machine, with or without zero-trust in front;
# see docker_deployment.md, "Reaching the dashboard"), set this host's LAN
# address and point the route at http://<LAN address>:UMAMI_CONSOLE_PORT.
# Change Umami's default admin / umami login first. Never 0.0.0.0 on a
# host with a public interface: the port is plain HTTP.
#UMAMI_CONSOLE_BIND=192.168.1.20

# Host port for the dashboard. Move it if something on this host already
# listens on 8081 (on any address): caddy cannot start otherwise, and caddy
# is the blog's only way in.
#UMAMI_CONSOLE_PORT=8081

# Where Caddy reaches Umami, for the tracker paths and the dashboard port.
# Unset, umami:3000, the Umami in this compose project. Once Umami runs with
# the back-office services elsewhere, its LAN address (http://host:port) --
# not its public hostname, or Umami counts the edge's address instead of
# each reader's.
#ANALYTICS_UPSTREAM=umami:3000

# Password of Pressroom's own Umami account, `pressroom`: an ordinary
# (non-admin) login it registers each blog's website with and reads numbers
# through, over the compose network. GENERATED: deploy.sh appends one
# (openssl rand -hex 24) the first time it finds none here, and the
# analytics-views one-shot creates or updates the account with it on every
# deploy. Leave it alone; changing it is a rotation (the next deploy applies
# it). deploy.sh fills in a missing or blank one, so this is not the way to
# switch analytics off -- PRESSROOM_ANALYTICS_UMAMI_APIURL below is.
#UMAMI_PRESSROOM_PASSWORD=

# Where Pressroom calls Umami's API. Unset (the default), the back-office
# overlay points it at http://umami:3000 on the stack's own network, and
# Pressroom registers every blog with Umami and shows its numbers. Set it
# BLANK -- uncomment the line below as it stands -- to switch analytics off:
# no tracker on any blog, no calls to Umami, and the Traffic pages say "not
# configured".
#PRESSROOM_ANALYTICS_UMAMI_APIURL=
#
# Once Umami runs with the back-office suite and the overlay is gone, set the
# address to Umami's LAN address, and the account's password directly (the
# overlay is what hands UMAMI_PRESSROOM_PASSWORD to the app today):
#PRESSROOM_ANALYTICS_UMAMI_APIURL=http://192.168.1.30:3000
#PRESSROOM_ANALYTICS_UMAMI_PASSWORD=

# The dashboard's address as YOU reach it -- its own hostname, e.g.
# https://umami.example.com -- for the "Open in Umami" links on Pressroom's
# Traffic pages. Blank, no link. Pressroom never calls it.
#PRESSROOM_ANALYTICS_UMAMI_DASHBOARDURL=

# Umami's own database server, umami-db (0.7.2+): its database name, its
# superuser, and that user's password. GENERATED: deploy.sh appends
# UMAMI_DB_PASSWORD (openssl rand -hex 24) the first time it finds none, and
# compose refuses to start without one. It is the server's initial password,
# so changing it here after the first start does NOT change it on the
# server; leave it alone. On an existing host the first 0.7.2 deploy copies
# Umami's data over from Pressroom's server by itself (umami-move).
UMAMI_DB=umami
#UMAMI_DB_USER=umami
#UMAMI_DB_PASSWORD=

# Filename Umami serves its tracker under. Renaming it from script.js is the
# cheapest way to stop content blockers matching on the path.
UMAMI_SCRIPT_NAME=script.js

# How long (seconds) the analytics-views one-shot waits for Umami's own
# website_event table to appear before giving up on installing the Grafana
# traffic view. The default of 180s comfortably covers a normal first boot
# (Umami's own Prisma migrations); raise it only on very slow storage. A
# timeout here costs nothing but the Grafana traffic view -- the blog, Umami
# and Listmonk are unaffected, and re-running
# `docker compose up -d analytics-views` once
# Umami has finished booting installs the view with no other side effects.
#ANALYTICS_VIEWS_WAIT_SECONDS=180

#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Newsletter (Listmonk)
#-----------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Listmonk owns the subscribers, the double opt-in flow and the unsubscribe
# links; Pressroom stores no subscriber data at all.
LISTMONK_ADMIN_USER=admin
LISTMONK_ADMIN_PASSWORD=changeme-generate-a-real-secret

# Hostname for the newsletter admin AND the opt-in/unsubscribe pages
# subscribers follow from email. Needs its own name: running listmonk under a
# sub-path is an open upstream feature request, not a supported configuration.
#
# LOCAL: ":8082", reachable at http://localhost:8082
#LISTMONK_DOMAIN=newsletter.example.com

# Host port for that LOCAL console; same rules as UMAMI_CONSOLE_PORT above.
# Moving it moves the "localhost:8082" in LISTMONK_ROOT_URL below too.
#LISTMONK_CONSOLE_PORT=8082

# Where Caddy reaches listmonk for LISTMONK_DOMAIN's site. Unset,
# listmonk:9000, the listmonk in this compose project; its LAN address
# (http://host:port) once it runs with the back-office services elsewhere.
#NEWSLETTER_UPSTREAM=listmonk:9000

# Must match LISTMONK_DOMAIN, with scheme and no trailing slash. Listmonk
# builds confirmation and unsubscribe links from this, and those links are the
# only way a subscriber can confirm or leave.
#
# LOCAL: http://localhost:8082
#LISTMONK_ROOT_URL=https://newsletter.example.com

# listmonk's own database server, listmonk-db (0.8.5+): its database name,
# its superuser, and that user's password. GENERATED: deploy.sh appends
# LISTMONK_DB_PASSWORD (openssl rand -hex 24) the first time it finds none,
# and compose refuses to start without one. It is the server's initial
# password, so changing it here after the first start does NOT change it on
# the server; leave it alone. On an existing host the first 0.8.5 deploy
# copies listmonk's data over from Pressroom's server by itself
# (listmonk-move).
LISTMONK_DB=listmonk
#LISTMONK_DB_USER=listmonk
#LISTMONK_DB_PASSWORD=

# NOTE: listmonk needs its own SMTP credentials to send anything. Those are set
# in its admin console (Settings -> SMTP), not here.

# Reader sign-in with Google (optional). Off unless both ID and SECRET are set.
# Redirect URI to register at the provider: <site absolute URL>/pressroom-ui/sso/callback
#PRESSROOM_SSO_OIDC_CLIENT_ID=
#PRESSROOM_SSO_OIDC_CLIENT_SECRET=
#PRESSROOM_SSO_OIDC_DISCOVERY_URI=https://accounts.google.com/.well-known/openid-configuration
#PRESSROOM_SSO_PROVIDER_LABEL=Google
